  "text": "Solution 2 is more likely correct, though I could not verify it against the paper.\n\n**Density exponent.** The density is transported, so in the moving frame the linearized equation gives ρ' = ρ₀'ψ/(U−c). Solution 1 treats this as ψ/(U−c) ~ ln|δ|, which would make the density as regular as the velocity. That holds only if ψ vanishes at the critical layer. A neutral mode with ψ(y_c) ≠ 0 gives ρ' ~ 1/δ instead. That is an inverse singularity, which lies in H^{−1/2−τ} but not H^{−1/2}, so s_ρ = −1/2. Solution 2's mechanism is therefore physically plausible.\n\n**Velocity exponent.** Near a critical layer, the streamfunction behaves like δ ln|δ|, so u = ψ_y ~ ln|δ|. That lies in H^{1/2−τ} but not H^{1/2}, so s_v = 1/2. Both solutions agree on this.\n\n**Why the exponents should differ.** The problem assigns separate critical exponents to velocity and density and asks for their sum. That framing suggests the density is rougher than the velocity, which matches s_ρ = −1/2. If both were 1/2, the question would be less natural.\n\nMy recollection is that the Lin–Xu abstract states velocity in H^{1/2−} and density in H^{−1/2−}, which would give a sum of 0. I am not certain of that recollection, and the density singularity analysis rests on the assumption ψ(y_c) ≠ 0, which I have not checked against the paper.\n\nCHOICE: 2",
